================================================================================
Third-party acknowledgement: Nault
================================================================================

The Nano key derivation implemented in `nano-keys` was written in Rust from the
published Nano specification and the published test vectors. No code was copied
from any other project.

Nault (https://github.com/Nault/Nault, https://nault.cc) was nevertheless the
decisive reference while validating it, and this project owes it a debt. Two
findings came directly from reading its source, and neither could be settled
from the specification alone:

1.  The Ed25519 expansion hash.

    A Nano private key is expanded to a signing scalar by hashing it. Standard
    Ed25519 — and therefore `tweetnacl`, which Nault's `package.json` lists as a
    dependency — uses SHA-512 for this step. The Nano specification uses
    Blake2b-512. These produce entirely different public keys from the same
    input, and both are "Ed25519-shaped", so the mistake is invisible without
    an external check.

    Nault's `package.json` is misleading here: the `nacl` it actually uses is
    not the npm package. `src/app/services/util.service.ts` resolves it as
    `window['nacl']`, which is loaded by a vendored fork at
    `src/assets/lib/tweetnacl/nacl.js`. That fork contains Blake2b and contains
    no SHA-512 anywhere, and its `derivePublicFromSecret` does exactly
    `blake2b(sk, 64)` followed by the standard clamp. That is what confirmed
    Blake2b-512 is correct and that the dependency list cannot be trusted to
    describe the code.

2.  The account index byte order.

    `generateAccountSecretKeyBytes` builds the index suffix with
    `hexToUint8(decToHex(accountIndex, 4))`, and `decToHex` pads on the *left*.
    That makes the index big-endian, matching docs.nano.org. This matters
    enormously: the account index is the one place where a plausible-looking
    bug produces a perfectly valid Nano address with a correct checksum that
    the user's seed will never regenerate. See ERRATA.md section 7.

Nault's source was also the explanation for a wallet behaviour that is not a bug
in this tool: `scanAccounts` in `wallet.service.ts` keeps only accounts that are
already used on the ledger, so importing a seed containing a brand-new empty
account appears to do nothing.

